<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moscow Peace Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Moscow Peace Treaty (1940) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> Peace Treaty, signed on 12 March 1940, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Finland kept its independence but ceded about a tenth of its territory, including the Karelian Isthmus with the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a>, lands north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a> and part of Salla, and leased the Hanko peninsula to the Soviets. Over 400,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> were evacuated from the ceded areas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow-peace-treaty.jpg" alt="Moscow Peace Treaty" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jniemenmaa · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Finnish_areas_ceded_in_1940.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> began on 30 November 1939 with the Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. On 29 January 1940 the Soviet foreign minister Vyacheslav Molotov dropped the puppet Terijoki Government and recognised the Ryti–Tanner government as the legal government of Finland, saying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> was willing to negotiate. The first tentative conditions reached Finland through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> two days later and were larger than the claims made before the war. They demanded the Karelian Isthmus, including the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a>, and Finland's shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a>, with the Hanko Peninsula leased for 30 years. Finland rejected them and appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the United Kingdom for troops.</p><p>In February 1940 the commander-in-chief, Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-gustaf-emil-mannerheim">Carl Gustaf Emil Mannerheim</a>, expressed pessimism about the military situation, and the government began peace talks on 29 February, the day the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> began an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>A Finnish delegation led by Prime Minister Risto Ryti travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 6 March, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> was close to surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a>. President Kyösti Kallio resisted giving up any territory but agreed to sign, and is said to have exclaimed: Let the hand wither that signs this monstrous treaty! The treaty was signed on the evening of 12 March, Moscow time, for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> by Molotov, Andrei Zhdanov and Aleksandr Vasilevsky and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> by Ryti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juho-kusti-paasikivi">Juho Kusti Paasikivi</a>, Rudolf Walden and Väinö Voionmaa.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> ceded approximately half of Finnish Karelia, more than the Soviets had demanded before the war. The area included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kexholm">Käkisalmi</a>, Sortavala, Suojärvi and the whole of Viipuri Bay, along with a part of the Salla area, the Finnish part of the Rybachi Peninsula and several islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a>. The Hanko Peninsula was leased to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> as a naval base for 30 years at an annual rent of 8 million marks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petsamo">Petsamo</a>, captured by the Russians during the war, stayed in Finnish hands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Troops and the remaining civilians were hastily evacuated inside the new border: 422,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a>, 12 per cent of the population, left their homes. The Soviet demand for rail transit rights to Hanko came only on 9 July, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> had allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> troops to cross its railways to occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> and the treaty were core factors leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continuation-war">Continuation War</a>, when hostilities resumed in 1941. The terms were not reversed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a>, and the debate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> over regaining the ceded territory is known as the Karelian question.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moscow Peace Treaty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Moscow Peace Treaty</b> of March 1940 ceded Viipuri and much of Karelia, about a tenth of the country, from which over 400,000 people were evacuated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Moscow Peace Treaty</b> of March 1940 ended the war, and the puppet government was quietly dissolved.</blockquote><a href="/state/finnish-democratic-republic">Finnish Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
